---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import ConcreteCalculator from '@components/islands/ConcreteCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'concrete-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How much concrete do I need?', answer: 'Pick the calculator that matches your pour — a slab, a round footing, a circular slab or tube, a curb and gutter run, or stairs — and enter its measurements. Each one returns the volume in cubic feet, cubic yards and cubic metres, the weight, and how many 60-lb and 80-lb bags that is.' },
  { question: 'How do I estimate concrete in cubic yards?', answer: 'Convert every measurement to feet first (a 4-inch slab is 4 ÷ 12 = 0.33 ft), multiply length × width × thickness for cubic feet, then divide by 27 for cubic yards. A 10 × 10 ft pad at 4 inches is 10 × 10 × 0.33 ÷ 27 ≈ 1.24 cubic yards.' },
  { question: 'How many bags of concrete are in a cubic yard?', answer: 'At the pre-mix density of 133 lbs per cubic foot, a cubic yard weighs about 3,591 lbs — roughly 45 × 80-lb bags or 60 × 60-lb bags. Because that is a lot of mixing, anything beyond about a cubic yard is usually cheaper and easier as ready-mix delivery.' },
  { question: 'How much does a cubic yard of concrete cost?', answer: 'This calculator estimates volume and bag counts, not cost. To budget, take the cubic yards it returns and multiply by your local price: ready-mix commonly runs in the region of $125–$175 per cubic yard delivered, though prices vary widely by supplier, mix and delivery conditions, and small loads carry a short-load fee. Bagged mix costs more per yard but suits jobs too small to order a truck.' },
  { question: 'How long does concrete take to dry and cure?', answer: 'You can usually walk on new concrete after 24–48 hours and drive on it after about 7 days, but it keeps hardening for weeks — reaching most of its rated strength at 28 days. Keeping it damp during that period (curing) makes the finished slab stronger.' },
  { question: 'Should I order more than the estimate?', answer: 'Yes — about 5–10% more. Some concrete is always lost to spillage, uneven subgrade and over-excavation, and running short mid-pour is far more costly than a little leftover, because a cold joint where the first pour has set is a permanent weakness.' },
  { question: 'What density does the estimate assume?', answer: 'Pre-mixed concrete at 2,130 kg/m³, or 133 lbs per cubic foot, which is the usual figure for a standard mix. Different types of concrete vary quite a bit — lightweight mixes are well below this and heavyweight aggregates well above — so treat the weight and bag counts as a close estimate rather than an exact figure.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} presentation="task-first" rail intro="Estimate concrete for a slab, footing, column, circular slab, curb and gutter run or stairs — in cubic feet, yards and metres, with the weight and the number of pre-mix bags." faqs={faqs} reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-25' }}>
  <ConcreteCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find the <strong>pour</strong> that matches your job — each of the five above is its own calculator, so an answer stays on screen while you work out the next.</li>
      <li>Enter each <strong>measurement</strong> and choose the unit beside it. Every measurement carries its own unit, so a slab measured in feet with a thickness in inches needs no converting by hand.</li>
      <li>Set the <strong>quantity</strong> if you are pouring several identical footings or slabs.</li>
      <li>Read the volume in <strong>cubic feet, cubic yards and cubic metres</strong>, then the weight and the number of 60-lb and 80-lb bags beneath it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Mixing units is fine and often quicker: a 16 ft by 10 ft pad at 4 inches thick can be entered
      exactly that way. Each measurement is converted to feet before the volume is worked out.
    </p>

    <h2>How concrete estimating works</h2>
    <p>
      Concrete is sold by volume, so every estimate comes down to turning a shape's dimensions
      into cubic yards. Work out the volume in cubic feet, then divide by 27 — there are 27 cubic
      feet in a cubic yard. The weight follows from the density of the mix, and the bag counts
      follow from the weight.
    </p>

    <h2>The five pours</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slabs, square footings or walls</strong> — length × width × thickness. The everyday rectangular pour.</li>
      <li><strong>Hole, column or round footings</strong> — a cylinder: π × (diameter ÷ 2)² × depth.</li>
      <li><strong>Circular slab or tube</strong> — a ring: the outer cylinder less its bore, π ÷ 4 × (d₁² − d₂²) × height.</li>
      <li><strong>Curb and gutter barrier</strong> — the flat gutter flag plus the curb standing on it, multiplied along the run.</li>
      <li><strong>Stairs</strong> — the platform slab plus a stack of treads. Each step up sits on the one below, so the concrete grows faster than the number of risers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>For dimensions measured in feet:</p>
    <p><strong>cubic yards = (length × width × depth) ÷ 27</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>length × width × depth</strong> — the volume in cubic feet, with depth in feet (a 4-inch slab is 0.333 ft).</li>
      <li><strong>÷ 27</strong> — converts cubic feet to cubic yards, the unit ready-mix is ordered in.</li>
      <li><strong>Weight</strong> — the volume times the density of the mix, 133 lbs per cubic foot (2,130 kg/m³) for standard pre-mix.</li>
      <li><strong>Bags</strong> — that weight divided by the weight of a bag, so a 60-lb bag goes further in count than an 80-lb one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Choose metres instead and the tool converts the dimensions to feet first, so the method
      and the 27-cubic-feet-per-yard constant stay the same underneath.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Say you are pouring a <strong>12 ft × 12 ft patio, 4 inches thick</strong>. Four inches
      is one-third of a foot, so the volume is 12 × 12 × 0.333 = <strong>48 cubic feet</strong>.
      Divide by 27 and that is <strong>1.78 cubic yards</strong>, or about <strong>1.36 cubic
      metres</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the standard pre-mix density that is 48 × 133 = <strong>6,384 lbs</strong>, or about
      <strong>80 × 80-lb bags</strong> and <strong>107 × 60-lb bags</strong> — a lot of hauling and
      mixing, which is exactly why a pour this size is usually ordered as ready-mix. Order 10% more
      than the estimate and it rises to roughly <strong>1.96 cubic yards</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Bags or ready-mix?</h2>
    <p>Bagged pre-mix suits small jobs — footings, fence posts, a single small pad. Each bag covers only a fraction of a cubic foot, so the count climbs fast:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Bag size</th><th>Yield per bag</th><th>Bags per cubic yard</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>60 lb</td><td>~0.45 cu ft</td><td>~60</td></tr>
        <tr><td>80 lb</td><td>~0.60 cu ft</td><td>~45</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>For anything much over a cubic yard, ordering ready-mix by the yard is usually cheaper and far less work than mixing dozens of bags by hand.</p>

    <h2>Order a little more than the estimate</h2>
    <p>
      Some concrete is always lost to spillage, an uneven subgrade and slight over-excavation,
      and running short mid-pour is far more costly than a little leftover. A margin of about
      <strong>5–10%</strong> covers it on a typical slab; add more for a rough or sloping site.
      The calculator reports exactly what the shape holds, so add that margin to the figure it
      gives you rather than expecting it to be built in.
    </p>

    <h2>How long concrete takes to set and cure</h2>
    <p>Fresh concrete is firm enough to walk on within 24–48 hours and strong enough to drive on after about a week, but it keeps gaining strength for weeks, reaching most of its rated value at <strong>28 days</strong>. Keeping the surface damp through the first days — curing — measurably increases the finished strength.</p>
    <p>These are estimates; confirm quantities with your supplier and allow for your own site conditions. For a room's floor area rather than a pour, see the <a href="/everyday/square-footage-calculator">square footage calculator</a>, or the <a href="/math/volume-calculator">volume calculator</a> for cylinders, columns and other shapes.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
